<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William I of Angoulême</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 962</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as William I Taillefer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, called Taillefer, was a 10th-century count of Angoulême in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Tradition gives him his nickname, 'iron-cutter', from a blow that split a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a>'s armour. His descendants, the Taillefer line, held the county for more than three centuries as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the dukes of Aquitaine.</p></div>
